  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nWidespread southerly wind gusts of 30 to 40 miles per hour are in \nplace this afternoon, with the strongest located over parts of \nnortheastern South Dakota and western Minnesota. These winds are \nexpected to persist through the afternoon before slowly diminishing \nthis evening. The strong winds combine with abnormally warm \ntemperatures (highs in the 70s to 80, approaching daily records) to \ncreate widespread elevated fire weather concerns this afternoon. \nHigh Grassland Fire Danger is in place across the entire forecast \narea, with some pockets of Very High Grassland Fire Danger over the \nparts of northeastern South Dakota where winds are expected to be \nthe strongest. \n\nA low pressure center to the north will drag a cold front across the \nforecast area early Saturday. Along this front, the next round of \nshowers to weak thunderstorms will develop. Central South Dakota may \nsee these showers during the early morning hours before the rain \nchances transition to northeastern South Dakota and western \nMinnesota after sunrise. Rain rates are generally expected to be \nlight, and only a couple hundredths of accumulation at most are \nexpected. \n\nPost-frontal winds turn from southerly to northwesterly and increase \novernight tonight and into early Saturday morning. Under the strong \ncold air advection regime, gusts upwards of 45 miles per hour (Wind \nAdvisory criteria) will be possible over parts of central and north \ncentral South Dakota. There is somewhat of a sharp difference in \nterms of guidance on this issue. The HREF and REFS seem to be less \nthan impressed by the setup, and show only a patchy 10-30% chance \nfor wind gusts to reach 45 miles per hour or better. The NBM on the \nother hand, shows a fairly widespread 30-60% chance over that same \narea. Have decided to err on the side of caution given the strength \nof the cold air advection behind the front, and a Wind Advisory is \nin effect late tonight into Saturday morning. Winds are expected to \ndecrease through the day, which is why the Advisory will expire at \n15Z Saturday morning. During that same time frame, there is some \npotential for strong downslope winds along the eastern edge of the \nPrairie Coteau out ahead of the front. Localized gusts may top out \nupwards of 40 miles per hour, but confidence is a bit lower in that \narea, and therefore no advisory has been issued for that area at \nthis time. \n\nTemperatures behind the cold front are set to cool back down closer \nto normal. 850mb temperatures drop about 15 degrees Celsius behind \nthe front, settling in the +2-5 degree range. These values are just \nbelow the median for this time of the year. As a result, expect near-\nnormal highs in the upper 40s to 50s for the foreseeable future. \n\nAn upper-level low will move up into the region, bringing the next \nchances for precipitation beginning on Sunday. Latest NBM \nprobabilities indicate that the chances of seeing a quarter of an \ninch through Tuesday morning ranges from 30-80%, increasing from \nnorth to south. There is also some indication that overnight lows \n(as well as temperatures through the whole profile) being sub-\nfreezing may support some flurries over parts of north central \nand/or northeastern South Dakota. However, no snowfall accumulation \nwould be expected out of any snow that does manage to reach the \nsurface. The presence of cloud cover may also limit radiational \ncooling through the night, keeping temperatures on the warmer side, \nwhich would be enough to shut down the prospective flurries.",
